# S12.1 R7 private-group Worktree barrier diagnosis — 2026-10-03

## Observed release result

The sole immutable R7 invocation authenticated successfully, released the
pristine legacy repository barrier and passed the read-only predeploy gate. It
then stopped fail-closed with `S12_1_RECOVERY_WORKTREE_BARRIER_RED` before a
backup, durable deployment-attempt marker or repository sync. Canonical
Application and Control remained clean at their predeploy commits.

## Deterministic cause

The exact R7 controller was executed read-only against the canonical paths.
`_capture_worktree_write_barrier()` stopped at `_worktree_barrier_mode()`.
Application had no rejected barrier entry. Control had exactly 30 rejected
tracked regular files: 25 at `0600` and 5 at `0700`. All tracked entries and
their tracked-path ancestors matched the recorded `chatops:chatops` owner; the
rejection was exclusively loss of owner read/execute access after the planned
root ownership handoff.

The original R8 draft attempted to treat the retained primary group as a
private authorization boundary. Final review correctly rejected that proof:
NSS enumeration need not be complete, and a setgid executable elsewhere on the
host could grant the numeric GID after a point-in-time process scan.

## Bounded correction

R8 preserves the no-write barrier and exact rollback contract without using a
GID as an authorization capability. When existing public permissions do not
preserve the former owner's read/execute class, it creates one exact temporary
POSIX access-ACL entry for the recorded numeric UID. The group-object entry
keeps its original read/execute permissions; the visible group-class bits are
only the ACL mask. Thus `0600` and `0700` still appear as temporary root-owned
`0440` and `0550`, but a shared group member or a process launched through an
unrelated setgid executable receives no new access.

Canonical Application paths already carry a versioned access ACL. The
controller preserves such an ACL only when the recorded numeric UID has an
explicit named-user entry whose effective read/execute permissions remain
sufficient under the existing, write-stripped mask. Group entries are never
used to prove owner access and the mask is never widened. ACL-free Control
paths instead receive the byte-exact generated owner ACL after write removal
and chown. The original normalized xattr fingerprint remains authoritative;
only the exact generated temporary ACL may be omitted while its barrier is
active. Teardown removes only a generated ACL and restores exact journaled UID,
GID and mode. Same-inode refresh and failure-path reseal enforce the same
contract. Interrupted states with the exact source fingerprint are safely
resealed or restored; any foreign ACL or xattr drift remains RED.

NSS account enumeration and live GID scans are therefore no longer security
inputs. Setuid and setgid regular files, write permissions, owner/ACL/xattr
drift, hardlinks and special files remain fail-closed. No runtime boundary,
provider policy or hard gate changes.
